# System (non-vendor) executables.
# goodixfingerprintd is a /system/bin daemon, so it must NOT be vendor_file_type.
# It reuses the platform fingerprintd domain rather than defining a new one:
# Pie still ships system/sepolicy/{public,private}/fingerprintd.te and that
# domain already carries binder_use, keystore access, fingerprintd_data_file
# and permission_service:find — everything a legacy fingerprint daemon needs.
# The label matters even though goodixfpd.rc also sets `seclabel`: the domain's
# entrypoint permission is on fingerprintd_exec, so under enforcing an
# unlabelled (system_file) binary would fail to exec. m681 never added this and
# only gets away with it because that port is permissive.
/system/bin/goodixfingerprintd  u:object_r:fingerprintd_exec:s0
# 2026-09-09, enforcing lane: on 18.1 this line matches NOTHING. The blob is
# installed to /vendor/bin (vendor-18.1/meizu/m95/m95-vendor.mk:19), and the
# live denial says so:
#   avc: denied { entrypoint } path="/vendor/bin/goodixfingerprintd"
#        scontext=u:r:fingerprintd:s0 tcontext=u:object_r:vendor_file:s0
# Deliberately NOT fixed by relabelling the vendor path: fingerprintd_exec is
# system_file_type (public/fingerprintd.te:2) and fingerprintd is a coredomain
# (private/fingerprintd.te), so neither the label nor a vendor_file execute
# grant is legal under public/domain.te:1328. The daemon needs a vendor domain
# of its own -- full recipe in SELINUX_ENFORCING_PLAN.md §4.2. Under enforcing
# as it stands today the daemon does not start and the fingerprint dies, so
# this is a hard blocker, not a cosmetic one.

